        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Peel and cut 600g of floury potatoes into 4cm chunks. Cut 2 medium onions into thin wedges. Set both aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the sesame oil in a large saucepan over medium-high heat. Add the pork slices and cook for 2 to 3 minutes, stirring, until browned. Do not crowd the pan."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the onion wedges to the pan. Stir-fry for 2 minutes until softened at the edges."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the potato chunks, then pour in the"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "dashi"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", soy sauce,"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "mirin"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", sake, and sugar. Stir to combine."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring to a boil. Skim any foam from the surface. Reduce to a steady simmer and cover with a lid. Cook for 20 to 25 minutes until the potato is completely tender when pierced."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Taste the broth and adjust with a little more soy sauce if needed. Serve in deep bowls with steamed rice on the side. Scatter sliced spring onions over the top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Potatoes remain hard after 25 minutes",
              "cause": "The heat was too low or the potatoes were cut too large.",
              "fix": "Ensure the liquid is at a steady simmer, not just a gentle steam. Cut potatoes no larger than 4cm. If still hard, continue simmering with the lid on for 10 more minutes."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Broth tastes too sweet",
              "cause": "The mirin and sugar together can over-sweeten the dish.",
              "fix": "Reduce the mirin to 40ml and omit the sugar on the next attempt. Taste the broth before adding the potato and adjust before the long simmer."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Pork is dry and stringy",
              "cause": "Pork shoulder sliced too thick requires longer cooking to become tender.",
              "fix": "Slice pork as thinly as possible, no more than 3mm. Alternatively, briefly freeze the pork for 20 minutes before slicing for cleaner, thinner cuts."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Nikujaga has a contested origin story. Both the city of Maizuru in Kyoto Prefecture and Kure in Hiroshima claim it was invented by a naval cook in the Meiji era, inspired by the British beef stew eaten by Japanese naval officers studying abroad. Whatever its true origin, the dish became a fixture of Japanese home cooking through the 20th century. Today it is eaten across all regions, with the main variation being pork in the west and beef in the east."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
